{
  "source": "https://walkthethames.co.uk/thames-path-distances/",
  "extracted": "2026-08-24",
  "note": "Settlements, landmarks, locks and named bridges from the walkthethames guide, source → Teddington (~152 miles). This is our curated whitelist of 'sensible waypoints' along the river — the strip view surfaces these plus locks from launches.json. Amenity fields join with data/curated-transport.json by 'name' → transport is not duplicated here.",
  "caveats": {
    "tier_is_soft": "The tier field ('major' vs 'minor') is the summariser's read of the guide's editorial styling — bold text, phrases like 'grand town' or 'busy place', amount of description. Two extraction passes disagreed on a handful (Purley on Thames, Bourne End, Teddington, Kingston). Trust the field for a broad-brush filter but expect to override individual entries by hand.",
    "kind_is_soft": "Similarly the kind classification (town/village/hamlet/lock/bridge/landmark) is a best-fit from the summariser. Some entries could plausibly go either way — 'Radley Boat House' as landmark, 'Hampton Court' as landmark rather than town.",
    "amenity_nulls": "Amenity booleans are true ONLY where the guide's prose explicitly mentions it. null means the guide is silent, NOT that the amenity is absent. Reading, Kingston, Windsor etc. obviously have pubs/shops/cafés but the guide's short description didn't itemise them, so they're null here. If you want a definitive 'has X within Y metres', use OSM as a supplemental layer.",
    "path_mi_vs_river": "path_mi is walker's distance along the Thames Path from source. It is NOT the same as our along_m (paddler's distance along the river centreline). resolve-curated.mjs will attach along_m by snapping each waypoint's OSM coordinates to the centreline."
  },
